  "slug": "maternal-remodeling-efae",
  "summary": "Mouse and goat studies now link hormonal control of intestinal immunity and lactation-stage tissue states to maternal-to-neonatal cell transfer.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is reproductive physiology reshaping maternal tissues and their immune compartments. In a mouse preprint, pregnancy-driven estradiol signaling expands intestinal regulatory T cells that secrete IL-10 and promote stem cell proliferation, remodeling the small intestine. A foster-nursing mouse preprint shows that maternal immune cells survive neonatal gut transit, enter circulation, and reach organs and injury sites in offspring. A single-cell and spatial transcriptomic preprint of Saanen goat mammary tissue across dry and peak lactation describes period-specific epithelial, stromal and immune changes. The goat study is descriptive profiling, and the transfer study concerns offspring rather than maternal remodeling, so the shared thread is partial. What is new is the joint attention to hormonal control of intestinal immune-epithelial remodeling, maternal-to-neonatal cell transfer, and lactation-stage mammary profiling.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-23"
}
